function rLearn(){$('learn').innerHTML=head('Learn','Everything you need to read this site','Plain-language definitions, sources and limits. Nothing here assumes expert knowledge.')+guide('learn')+`<h2 style="margin-top:20px">Words you will meet</h2><div class="g">${(LANG=='hi'?GLH:GL).map(g=>`<div class="c"><h3>${g[0]}</h3><p class="sm m" style="margin:0">${g[1]}</p></div>`).join('')}</div>
<h2>Where the numbers come from</h2><div class="g2"><div class="c"><h3>Statewide history</h3><p class="sm m">Crop-wise Area, Production & Yield workbook (DA&amp;FW), 1966–67 to 2024–25, season Total. The latest year is a third advance estimate.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>District records</h3><p class="sm m">state_apy_2024_25.xlsx: 1,596 records, 38 districts, 32 crop labels. Sub-crops and seasons are summed and yield is recomputed as production ÷ area; the file’s own yield column has 8 blanks and text errors, and its provenance is not stated.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>All-states comparison</h3><p class="sm m">india_all_crops_2024_25.xlsx. Used for ranking only. For Bihar, the state’s own records govern.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Which number wins?</h3><p class="sm m">For Bihar, Bihar’s own records (the state workbook) govern. The statewide series and the national file are the same data for Bihar; they are used for the long history and for comparing states. They differ most in the latest, advance-estimate years.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Climate and prices</h3><p class="sm m">Statewide annual and seasonal rainfall and temperature, and nominal MSP series, from the supplied files.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Boundaries</h3><p class="sm m">Current 38 district outlines from a public GeoJSON file, simplified for display. Historical districts are not mapped onto them.</p></div><div class="c"><h3>Articles and reports</h3><p class="sm m">${EV.length} sources found by web search about Bihar’s droughts, floods, heat and maize shift. Each is summarised in our own words and linked.</p></div></div>
<h2>How the AI answers are built</h2><div class="c"><p>When you ask the AI about a data point, it is given the computed numbers and the matching sources and asked to sort what it says into three groups: <b>Documented</b> (supported by a cited source), <b>Possible explanations</b> (plausible, not established) and <b>Unknown</b>. A rise or fall is an observation, not proof of cause. The answer changes with your reading mode and can be requested in Hindi.</p><p class="sm m" style="margin:0">${AI_OK?'This build calls a small NVIDIA NIM model and searches the live web through your own server.':'The AI assistant is switched off in this copy. It can be enabled by deploying the Worker and setting AI_URL in config.js (see README).'}</p></div>
<h2>Limits to keep in mind</h2><div class="take w2"><ul class="sm" style="margin:0"><li>Statewide averages can hide local shocks such as a single flood.</li><li>District data is for one year, 2024–25; there is no district time series here.</li><li>Climate is statewide and calendar-year, so seasonal timing is approximate.</li><li>The platform is not an official statistics system and not farm advice.</li></ul></div>`}

