Object.assign(GUIDE,{districts:[['How to read the district panels','Districts differ in what they grow. **Crop diversity** shows how many different crops a district really relies on. **Specialisation** shows which crop a district is unusually strong in. **Farming types** groups districts that grow similar crops. **Yield gap** shows how much more could be harvested if districts with low harvest per hectare reached a better level. These are comparisons, not scores.'],['Methods','All panels use the 2024–25 state APY records (1,596 rows, 38 districts), summed over seasons and sub-crops. Diversity is exp(Shannon entropy) of area shares; specialisation is a location quotient; types come from seeded k-means on area shares; yield gap is an arithmetic gap to a target yield. None is a causal or forecasting model.'],['Using this to target','Diversity flags exposure to single-crop shocks; specialisation shows where crop-specific support reaches the most farmers; farming types suggest groupings for extension and inputs; yield gaps show headroom in tonnes. Treat gaps as hypotheses: crop mix, irrigation, season and reporting can all explain a low yield.']],
seasons:[['Bihar’s farming seasons','Bihar’s crops follow three main seasons. **Kharif** crops are sown with the monsoon, **rabi** crops in winter, and **summer** crops between. Bihar’s own district records use four names: **Aghani** (main kharif, winter rice), **Bhadhai** (early kharif), **Garma** (summer) and **Rabi**. Pick a crop to see which season it grows in, and how that changed.'],['Sources and limits','Seasonal rows come from the DA&FW statewide workbook; the district season names from the state APY file. A season with no reported area is shown as zero, and seasonal totals may not equal the Total series where the source reports only a total.'],['Why seasons matter for planning','Season shares tell you when demand for water, labour, storage and procurement peaks. A shift between seasons, as with maize moving toward rabi, changes irrigation and market timing needs, so check it before planning support.']],
structure:[['When and why: three questions','This page asks: **When did yield start rising faster?** **Did farmers shift between cereals, pulses and oilseeds?** **Did harvests grow because farmers planted more land, or because each hectare gave more?** The answers describe patterns; they do not prove what caused them.'],['Methods','Break points come from a two-segment OLS fit with one breakpoint (minimum squared error); rolling growth is a 10-year CAGR; the decomposition splits log production growth into area and a yield residual. These are descriptive screens with no significance test and no causal claim.'],['Reading growth responsibly','Separate extensive growth (more land) from intensive growth (higher yield), and watch whether the mix drifts away from pulses and oilseeds. Compare break years with known policy, seed or irrigation changes before attributing anything.']],
compare:[['Two things, side by side','Pick two crops or two districts. For crops, both lines start at 100 in the first year both have data, so you can see which grew more. For districts, the bars show how much land each gives to each crop.'],['Method notes','Crops are indexed to 100 at the first common year; the correlation uses year-on-year changes. District yields are derived from the 2024–25 APY records. Aggregates and single crops are different definitions.'],['Using comparisons','Where two crops or districts differ most, ask what explains it: irrigation, seed, season mix, price, or reporting. A comparison suggests where to look, not what is right.']]});
GL.push(['Aghani, Bhadhai, Garma, Rabi','Bihar’s district records name four seasons: Aghani is the main kharif season (winter rice), Bhadhai the early kharif (autumn crops), Garma the summer season and Rabi the winter-sown season.'],['Effective number of crops','A measure of crop diversity: how many equally sized crops would give the same mix. Higher means more spread out.'],['Location quotient','A district’s share of land in a crop divided by the state’s share. Above 1 means the district specialises in it.'],['Yield gap','How far a district’s yield is below a target such as the state average, multiplied by its area to give tonnes.'],['Structural break','A year after which a trend changes pace, found by fitting two straight lines. It describes a change, not its cause.'],['k-means cluster','A way of grouping districts with similar crop mixes. The groups depend on the number chosen.']);

